# UNITY'S GENERATED PROJECTS, AND ONLY THOSE. The leading slash anchors this
# to `ledger/`, which is where Unity writes `Assembly-CSharp.csproj` and
# friends. Everything in a subdirectory is a hand-written tool and is code.
#
# THIS WAS `*.csproj` PLUS A HAND-KEPT ALLOWLIST OF FOUR NEGATIONS, and the
# allowlist did exactly what allowlists do. `ReachCheck.csproj` was written,
# committed as far as I could tell, and silently dropped — so CI ran
# `dotnet run --project ledger/ReachCheck` against a directory holding a
# Program.cs and no project, and every core-tests run since has been red with
# "Couldn't find a project to run". BalanceLab and BarkGen were dropped the
# same way and nobody noticed, because nothing in CI had run them yet.
#
# A rule that has to be edited every time a tool is added is a rule that will
# be forgotten every time a tool is added. This one cannot be.
/*.csproj
/*.sln

# Generated playtest report
SimHarness/sim-report.md

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Crash reports
sysinfo.txt
*.stackdump

# Secrets — API keys live in local untracked config, never in git
Assets/StreamingAssets/secrets.json

# .NET tool build output
Tier2Gen/bin/
Tier2Gen/obj/

# ShapeCheck build output (the tool is source + csproj only)
ShapeCheck/bin/
ShapeCheck/obj/

# GameCheck build output. The project and its four uGUI shims are source and
# are tracked; the compiled Unity reference assemblies come from NuGet on
# demand and must never be committed.
GameCheck/bin/
GameCheck/obj/
